result.loop_seamless:
Read aloud, last line into first: "...The fleets never saw each other. They fought with planes, from far out at sea. That's why the carriers sunk at Midway aren't at Midway. In June, nineteen forty-two..." It is grammatical and natural, and the replay would be invisible. "That's why" plus the hook is an ordinary sentence. Cutting both pieces from one take at the 80-150 ms gap between "why" and "the" keeps the real breath across the seam.
The picture loop is the best-engineered part of the draft:
- frames 882-899 are rendered from H(t-30);
- the camera pose, sun and card are the same at the seam;
- water and foam run on a 30.000 s periodic clock;
- the mix is mastered as a loop;
- an SSIM of 0.97 or more is required.
Three risks:
(a) The 0.8 s silent beat before "That's why" signals the end, so many viewers leave before the loop sentence starts. Cut it to 0.25-0.35 s and read "from far out at sea" with continuing, not falling, intonation.
(b) The hook piece is cut from the paragraph-final sentence of the take. Generate the take with the opening sentences repeated after it (see swipe notes) so both sides of the seam have mid-paragraph prosody.
(c) At 29.4 the line retracts to the reef and redraws. That is a visible mini-reset 0.57 s before the seam. It is fine as a rewind motif, but keep the stop under 3 frames so it doesn't read as a jump.
Also, YouTube's loop can add a small playback hitch. A natural 110 ms word gap absorbs this better than a hard sentence join would. Test on a real phone after upload, not only in the NLE.

Read aloud, it passes: "...And that turtle has no idea | this beach is on a mountain almost twice as tall as Everest. It's Mauna Loa..." It is grammatical, natural English (a "has no idea [that]..." clause), and on a replay the seam is invisible. The frames are planned correctly:
- the same POSE A on both sides of the seam;
- the surf and turtle flutter on a 5.40 s clock with 6 cycles in 32.40 s;
- the card settled by 31.80;
- music silent from 31.0 to 0.4, and no SFX crossing the seam;
- the crane decelerating to rest, then easing away from rest (a natural hang point).
Gaps to close:
(1) Velocity 0 at 32.40 is specified for position only. The dolly-zoom also changes vfov (12° to 34°), so the vfov rate and the pitch rate must also reach 0 at the seam, or the replay shows a lens "breathing" jump.
(2) "Retime every anchor to the TTS word timings": if the real take changes the total length, the loop clock must be recomputed (surf period = total / 6), and the surf bed re-cut to the new length.
(3) The seam's prosody: the plan adds 0.25 s of silence between "idea" and "this", inside a clause that in the original take probably had little or no pause. Test both directions: the end-to-start join on 5 loops, and the start heard cold (see swipe risk). If you regenerate VO1 separately for a stronger opener, the seam is no longer one take. Then test that "has no idea | THIS beach" does not jump in pitch or loudness.

result.loop_check:
AUDIO: the last line followed by the first line, as one passage:
"Then the ice melted, the sea crept back, and ever since, you can't walk to Lānaʻi. Unless you pull the plug."
- It is plain English: a "since" clause completed by a main clause. On a first view, "You can't walk to Lānaʻi." still stands alone.
- On replay it also closes a joke circle. The only plug that ever drained this sea was the Ice Age (the 12.99 line), and the sea came back when the ice melted.
- Both halves come from ONE TTS take, with the text rotated. The cut sits inside the take's own comma pause after "since,". Rodney's comma pauses measure 0.08-0.15 s in audio/rodney_test/timing.json. At most 0.1 s of room tone is added, giving about 0.16 s at the end and 0.05 s at the start. No other added silence and no time-stretching. The prosody, breath and room tone therefore continue across the seam.
- Music: 96 BPM, 10 bars = 25.000 s. Bar 10's chase resolves on 24.375 into the caper pickup, which lands on bar 1's downbeat at frame 0.
- The surf bed is a loop.
- The refill audio (the reversed gurgle) ends at 24.375, when the plug seats. From 24.45 the bed is surf, swash and the pickup, which is identical to the bed at frame 0. There is no rumble, no roar and no fade.
- Export:
  - exactly 750 frames and exactly 25.000 s of audio;
  - AAC encoder priming trimmed and checked with ffprobe;
  - played looped for 3 cycles on a phone;
  - plus one cold start from 0.00, to confirm VO1 sounds like an opening. Pick the take that passes both checks.

PICTURE: last frame vs first frame.
- Frames 684-749 (22.80-24.967 s) and frames 0-135 (0-4.50 s) are ONE continuous render of Shot A, from render time t = -2.2 to 4.5.
- Frame 749 is t = -0.033 and frame 0 is t = 0. They are consecutive frames of the same render, so the seam is not a cut at all, hidden or otherwise. That removes the draft's G2-to-pre-roll hidden cut.
- Everything matches by construction:
  - the camera is on the frame-0 push path, at the same speed since the whip landed at 22.80;
  - the tourist's pose is the same: a 12° brace, right fist on the ring, suitcase in his left hand, front foot in the swash;
  - the chain is taut and the plug is seated;
  - the swash touches his foot at the seam;
  - the ferry's position and bob phase are the same;
  - the LĀNAʻI and MAUI slips are at identical positions;
  - the cloud shadows have been at normal speed since 24.60;
  - same sun and grade, and no other text.
- The only cut near the end is the visible whip-pan at 22.55-22.80, 2.2 s before the seam.
- Everything that differs from frame 0 (the ferry righting itself, the slip returns, the landing, the plug, the settle) finishes by 24.80, with at least 0.15 s between events. From 24.85 to 25.00 only the swash runs up.
- Gate: the frame-749-to-frame-0 SSIM must be no lower than the shot's median consecutive-frame SSIM.
